      <!-- Section 2: GHA vs Registry Architecture -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white tracking-tight mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="text-emerald-400 font-mono">02.</span> GHA vs Registry Cache Backends
        </h2>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 my-6">
          <div class="bg-slate-900/80 border border-slate-800 p-5 rounded-xl">
            <h3 class="text-emerald-400 font-bold text-base mb-2 font-mono flex items-center gap-2">
              <span>●</span> type=gha (Actions Cache)
            </h3>
            <ul class="text-xs text-slate-300 space-y-2 list-disc list-inside">
              <li><strong>Zero Credential Friction:</strong> Authenticates seamlessly using native GitHub Action workflow tokens without external secrets.</li>
              <li><strong>Hyper-Localized Transport:</strong> Layer blobs are served directly from Azure/AWS internal caching storage collocated with GitHub-hosted runners.</li>
              <li><strong>Scope Isolation:</strong> Branch scoping rules apply: PR workflows can access caches from the base/default branch (<code class="text-slate-200">main</code>), preventing cache pollution across feature branches.</li>
              <li><strong>10 GB Quota Limit:</strong> Repository cache is capped at 10 GB; aggressively evicted via Least-Recently-Used (LRU).</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-slate-900/80 border border-slate-800 p-5 rounded-xl">
            <h3 class="text-cyan-400 font-bold text-base mb-2 font-mono flex items-center gap-2">
              <span>●</span> type=registry (OCI / GHCR)
            </h3>
            <ul class="text-xs text-slate-300 space-y-2 list-disc list-inside">
              <li><strong>Unlimited Storage Quota:</strong> Registry caching is bound only by your registry storage tier (GitHub Packages GHCR, AWS ECR, Quay.io).</li>
              <li><strong>Universal Portability:</strong> Self-hosted runners in Kubernetes or bare-metal clusters can pull the exact same cache layers generated on cloud runners.</li>
              <li><strong>Multi-Branch Durability:</strong> Caches persist indefinitely until explicitly pruned via container registry lifecycle policies.</li>
              <li><strong>Requires Registry Auth:</strong> Needs explicit login actions with write permissions to publish cache manifest artifacts.</li>
            </ul>
